67
00:42:27.000 --> 00:43:09.000
which justify doing harm on the basis of the greater good and identifying with a greater good cause. So as I say, we hope that our work and our reinterpretation of that incredibly powerful phenomenon that Milgram displayed to us will make us more aware of when we too are following those dangerous steps to doing harm in the name of the greater good. Let me close by asking you about the coronation that you just attended since you spent so many hours out there in the rain. Did you see anything that you didn't expect?
它们以更大的善和认同更大的善为由，为主张造成伤害的行为进行辩解。所以正如我所说，我们希望我们的工作以及对米尔格拉姆向我们展示的那个极其强大的现象的重新诠释，能让我们更加警惕：什么时候我们也会以更大的善的名义，步入那些导致伤害的危险步伐。最后，我想问你关于你刚刚参加的加冕典礼的问题，既然你在雨中呆了那么长时间。你有没有看到什么意料之外的事情？

68
00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:50.000
I mean, clearly you were there studying as well as participating. What was that like for you and what were your observations? Well, you know, I love crowds. And one of the things I always say about being an academic is, you know, the great thing about an academic life is people pay you to study about things that you find in. So I find crowds fascinating. And so I can go to a football crowd. I can go to a political rally. I can go to the coronation and I can say I'm collecting data. And that's a huge privilege and it's a huge pleasure. See, I started my study of crowds. In 1980, I was doing my PhD, doing my doctorate, sitting in a lab, doing social influence experiments when the first of the
我的意思是，显然你在那里既是去研究也是去参与。那对你来说是什么体验？你有什么观察？嗯，你知道的，我喜欢人群。关于成为一名学者，我总是说的一点是，学术生活最棒的地方在于人们付钱让你去研究你觉得有趣的事情。所以我发现人群非常迷人。因此我可以去一个足球观众群。我可以去一个政治集会。我可以去参加加冕典礼，然后可以说我在收集数据。这是一种巨大的特权，也是一种巨大的快乐。你看，我开始研究人群是在1980年。当时我正在攻读博士学位，坐在实验室里做社会影响实验，这时1980年代的英国城市骚乱爆发了，

69
00:43:50.000 --> 00:44:44.000
British urban riots of the 1980s happened, St. Paul's riots. And I found it fascinating. And It so challenged these notions of mad mob, which were in all the newspapers the next day, that it set me on a course of study and I'm still going there. But for most of my life, I've been interested in protest crowds and how crowds challenge the status quo. But what we underestimate, I think, is the role of, if you like, establishment crowds, crowds which affirm the status quo, crowds which help create the identities by which we live our lives. So one thing that fascinated me, I studied the crowds around the Queen's mourning, the Queen's death, then the coronation. And they, I think, are very important in helping create
圣保罗骚乱。我发现这太迷人了。它极大地挑战了第二天所有报纸上都在鼓吹的“疯狂暴徒”观念，以至于它让我踏上了一条研究之路，并且我一直坚持到现在。但在我生命的大部分时间里，我一直对抗议人群以及人群如何挑战现状感兴趣。但我认为，我们低估了那种所谓的建制人群的作用，即那些肯定现状的人群，那些帮助塑造我们生活认同感的人群。所以有一件事让我着迷，我研究了女王哀悼期间、女王去世时以及随后的加冕典礼周围的人群。我认为它们对于创造

70
00:44:44.000 --> 00:45:34.000
a sense of Britishness and to redefine that sense of Britishness. So although not everybody who went to the morning, and although not everybody who went to the coronation was a royalist, many people were there because it was an interesting thing. It was a historic event. They went to be with others. They went because they were tourists. It was seen as a huge crowd of people who supported the monarchy and therefore affirmed a particular version of Britishness. At a time where Britain is not doing desperately well in the world post-Brexit, it gave a sense of pride, look at the way we choreographed these things, look at the ways in which we're linked to history, look at the ways in which other countries look at us, and in that sense,
一种英国人身份认同感以及重新定义这种英国人身份认同感非常重要。因此，尽管并非所有去参加哀悼的人、并非所有去参加加冕典礼的人都是保皇派，但许多人去那里是因为这是一件有趣的事情。这是一场历史事件。他们去是为了和别人在一起。他们去是因为他们是游客。它被视为一大群支持君主制的人，因此肯定了某种特定版本的英国身份。在一个英国在脱欧后的世界里过得并不是特别好的时期，它带来了一种自豪感：看看我们是如何策划这些事情的，看看我们与历史的联系方式，看看其他国家是如何看待我们的，从这个意义上说，

71
00:45:34.000 --> 00:46:16.000
it increased the sense of national identification, the sense of national pride. So I am fascinated, and we've collected the data. We haven't fully analysed it yet, because it only happened a few days ago in the ways in which crowds not only act on the basis of pre-existing identities, but the way in which crowds form the everyday identities by which we live. You see, the notion of crowds by and large has been they are an eruption of something primitive into contemporary life. They are like Victorian freak shows. They're fascinating, but they're not really relevant to the real stuff of psychology and
它增强了国民认同感和民族自豪感。所以我很着迷，而且我们已经收集了数据。我们还没有完全分析它，因为它在几天前才刚刚发生。我们关注人群不仅根据预先存在的身份行事的方式，还关注人群塑造我们赖以生存的日常身份的方式。你看，人们对人群的普遍看法是，它们是某种原始事物在当代生活中的爆发。它们就像维多利亚时代的畸形秀。它们很迷人，但与心理学的真正核心内容并没有太大关系，

72
00:46:16.000 --> 00:47:03.000
society. For me, crowds both theoretically, but also practically, tell you about those basic processes which define who we are and how we relate to others and what we should do and how we feel. And I think in studying coronation crowds and mourning crowds and the ways in which these crowds help consolidate a particular understanding of our national identities, that contributes their understanding that crowd psychology should be very much at the center of psychology and the center of this understanding of society. Well, I think you've just made a very compelling case, and I want to thank you for joining me today. Dr. Reischer, it's been really interesting. It's been great to talk to you. Thank you.
社会也是如此。对伞兵、人群来说，无论是从理论上还是从实践上，它们都向你展示了那些定义了我们是谁、我们如何与他人建立联系、我们应该做什么以及我们如何感受的基本过程。我认为，通过研究加冕典礼人群、哀悼人群，以及这些人群如何帮助巩固对我们国家认同感的某种特定理解，这有助于深化这样一种认知：人群心理学应该非常坚定地处于心理学中心，并成为理解社会的中心。嗯，我想你刚刚做了一个非常令人信服的陈述，我要感谢你今天加入我们。瑞彻博士，这真的很有趣。与你交谈太棒了。谢谢你。

